  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nShower and thunderstorm potential this afternoon and evening\n\nThis afternoon into tonight, shortwave currently moving over SD \nrolls eastward to northern WI. As heights aloft cool ahead of the \nwave, steep mid-level lapse rates will develop leading to the \npotential for a shower or thunderstorm in the northern parts of our \nCWA, north of I-94. That said, warm temperatures in the 700-800mb \nlayer look to both limit overall instability and thunderstorm \npotential and to keep any activity elevated. Thus, while shear \nprofiles through the column would be supportive of all forms of \nsevere weather, shear through the effective layer of any uncapped \nparcels is far less supportive. Thus, other than lightning and \nperhaps a small hailstone, am not concerned about hazards with any \nthunderstorms that do develop.\n\nAdditional precipitation potential Thursday into Thursday night\n\nStill on track for an upper low to barrel over southern Nevada \nWednesday and phase with a northern stream trough Thursday with the \nresulting surface low quickly shifting northeast from the TX \nPanhandle toward Detroit. Guidance, which previously had large \ntiming differences, has focused on the Thursday/Thursday night \nperiod with remaining uncertainty surrounding the exact trajectory \nof the upper trough. With our forecast area on the northwestern \nperiphery of the surface low, final trajectory will be very \nimportant, particularly for locations across southeast MN and north \nof I-90 in WI. For areas in our southeast, am now carrying 80 PoPs \nacross parts of northeast IA and far southwest WI. Progged \n850/925/sfc temperatures in operational GFS/EC/CMC still look to \nremain above freezing during the event and 12.13z NBM, while having \na non-zero (specifically, 1%) chance for snow in parts of Taylor \nCounty, is pretty much all rain, so have continued with an all rain \nforecast.\n\nKilling freeze potential Friday night north of I-94\n\nBroad surface high builds in the wake of the Thursday night's upper \ntrough. Clearing skies and light winds after a period of post-\nfrontal CAA will very likely (70-100%) lead to widespread frost, \npotentially (30%+) a freeze and, for areas north of I-94, a chance \n(20-50%) for a killing freeze with temperatures below 28. With some \nguidance suggesting some surface pressure gradient and thus light \nwinds will be maintained Friday night will need to continue to \nmonitor this period as there is still some uncertainty with regards \nto temperatures ending up a few degrees warmer than currently \nforecast.",
